WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:04.370
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:11.620
In this work, a 121-layer convolutional neural
network is trained to recognize pneumonia

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.990
and 13 different diseases.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:19.590
Pneumonia is an inflammatory lung condition
that is responsible for a million hospitalizations

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:23.340
and 50,000 deaths per year in the US alone.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:28.350
Such an algorithm requires a training set
of formidable size to work properly.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.080
This means a bunch of input-output pairs.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:36.620
In this case, one training sample is an input
frontal X-ray image of the chest, and the

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:41.769
outputs are annotations by experts who mark
which of the 14 different sought diseases

00:00:41.769 --> 00:00:43.639
are present in this sample.

00:00:43.639 --> 00:00:47.819
So they say like this image contains pneumonia
here, and this doesn't.

00:00:47.819 --> 00:00:53.190
This is not just a binary yes or no answer,
but a more detailed heatmap of possible regions

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.930
that fit the diagnosis.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:01:01.600
The training set used for this algorithm contained
over a 100.000 images of over 30.000 patients.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.220
This is then given to the neural network,
and its task is to learn the properties of

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:08.000
these diseases by itself.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.510
Then, after the learning process took place,
previously unseen images are given to the

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.460
algorithm and a set of radiologists.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:21.330
This is called a test set, and of course,
it is crucial that both the training and the

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.210
test sets are reliable.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:28.630
If the training and test set is created by
one expert radiologist, and then we again

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:33.451
benchmark a neural network against a different,
randomly picked radiologist, that's not a

00:01:33.451 --> 00:01:38.990
very reliable process because each of the
humans may be wrong in more than a few cases.

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:45.220
Instead, the training and test annotation
data is created by asking multiple radiologists

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.930
and taking a majority vote on their decisions.

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:53.760
So now that the training and test data is
reliable, we can properly benchmark a human

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.470
versus a neural network.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:02:01.440
And here's the result: this learning algorithm
outperforms the average human radiologist.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.490
The performance was measured in a 2D space,
where sensitivity and specificity were the

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:08.819
two interesting metrics.

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:13.239
Sensitivity means the proportion of positive
samples that were classified as positive,

00:02:13.239 --> 00:02:18.129
and specificity means the portion of negative
samples that were classified as negative.

00:02:18.129 --> 00:02:23.269
The crosses mean the human doctors, and as
you can see, whichever radiologist we look

00:02:23.269 --> 00:02:28.829
at, even though they have different false
positive and negative ratios, they are all

00:02:28.829 --> 00:02:33.720
located below the blue curve which denotes
the results of the learning algorithm.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:39.190
This is a simple diagram, but if you think
about what it actually means, this is an incredible

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:41.629
application of machine intelligence.

00:02:41.629 --> 00:02:43.519
And now, a word on limitations.

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:48.969
It is noted that this was an isolated test,
for instance, the radiologists were only given

00:02:48.969 --> 00:02:54.499
one image, and usually, when diagnosing someone,
they know more about the history of the patient

00:02:54.499 --> 00:02:56.860
that may further help their decisions.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:01.909
For instance, a history of a strong cough
and high fever is highly useful supplementary

00:03:01.909 --> 00:03:06.200
information for humans when diagnosing someone
who may have pneumonia.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.859
Beyond only the frontal view of the chest,
it is also standard practice to use the lateral

00:03:10.859 --> 00:03:13.930
views as well if the results are inconclusive.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:18.270
These views are not available in this dataset
and it is conjectured that it may sway the

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.389
comparison towards humans.

00:03:20.389 --> 00:03:26.620
However, I'll note that this information may
also benefit the AI just as much as the radiologists,

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:29.700
and this seems like a suitable direction for
future work.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:34.540
Finally, this is not the only algorithm for
pneumonia detection, and it has been compared

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:40.709
to the state of the art for all 14 diseases,
and this new technique came out on top on

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:41.709
all of them.

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:47.489
Also, have a look at the paper for details
because training a 121-layer neural network

00:03:47.489 --> 00:03:51.290
requires some clever shenanigans as this was
the case here too.

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:56.239
It is really delightful to see that these
learning algorithms can help diagnosing serious

00:03:56.239 --> 00:04:01.129
illnesses, and provide higher quality healthcare
to more and more people around the world,

00:04:01.129 --> 00:04:05.510
especially in places where access to expert
radiologists is limited.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:07.499
Everyone needs to hear about this.

